00:00:01.200 --> 00:00:06.200 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  thinking about Buddhism as a religious  tradition requires us to keep in mind  it's deep roots as well as its wide  branches the roots of Buddhism stretch  back in time to about the 5th century  BCE a time of great social economic and  political change Buddhism's beginnings  were profoundly shaped by these  conditions a change in ancient India but  Buddhism also represents a distinctive  and innovative response to these same  conditions while Buddhism has its  historical roots in India reverence for  the Buddha Buddhist thought and practice  and Buddhist institutions spread first  throughout Asia and then across the  entire world thus to understand Buddhism  as a religion we always have to consider  its deep roots in time as well as its  wide branches in space Buddhists see the  origins of their religious traditions in  the life of a prince named Siddhartha  Gautama Buddha secants of his life  portray him as leaving behind his royal  status his family as well as his palace  to set out to find answers to deep  questions about suffering and its causes  as well as to find out whether freedom  from suffering and death is possible  after years of searching and effort he  discovered what he was seeking and this  discovery is encapsulated in the epithet  Buddha the awakened one the name Buddha  that no parent ever gave indicates that  the Buddha is to us as we are to people  who are asleep the name is a celebration  of his achievement on the basis of what  he discovered the Buddha began to teach  the Dharma truth with a capital T and  the diversity of Buddhist thought over  the ages across Asia represents the  efforts of countless men and women to  understand and realize this truth and  especially to understand and realize  what the Buddha taught us about reality  and human flourishing the Buddha not  only taught ideas but also a way of life  the Buddha established institutions that  grounded the community that developed  around him and teachings that provided  guidance to that community on how to  live so that its members could achieve  enlightenment themselves those who set  out to live as the Buddha taught are  called the Sangha from the beginning the  Buddha's disciples included both female  and male monastics as well as male and  female laypeople this way of life in  these institutions were enduring but  they also proved to be very adaptable to  new settings and circumstances as early  as the first century the tradition began  to travel over the Silk Road into West  and Central Asia and by the eighth  century  Buddhism was well established in China  Korea and Japan  Buddhism also spread south across India  to Sri Lanka and to Southeast Asia from  Burma to Vietnam and Java this spread of  Buddhism across Asia enriched the  Buddhist heritage by the addition of new  texts into the canons of Buddhist  scriptures and in this regard Buddhists  have had more open canons than have  Christians and Muslims in time some  Buddhist communities also developed dual  canons in which one portion of Scripture  was interpreted in the light of the  subsequent Canon of scriptures that was  deemed to be closer to the intentions of  the Buddha as a teacher Buddhist dual  canons are analogous to the combination  of the Old Testament and the New  Testament in the Christian Bible or the  Quran in relation to the texts of other  peoples of the book the Buddhist  heritage was also greatly enriched by  the translation of Buddhist scriptures  into many different languages including  Pali and Sanskrit in South and Southeast  Asia to Tibetan and Chinese in Central  and East Asia the translation of  Buddhist scriptures into different  languages was always an enormous project  since the canonical collections of texts  that carry the Buddha's teachings can  include thousands of individual texts  the complexity and diversity of Buddhist  thought and practice the richness of its  character in local settings are so  pronounced that when Europeans first  encountered Buddhists in their  explorations at the beginning of the  colonial age it took them quite a while  to realize that the religion they  encountered in sri lanka was  historically related to what they saw in  japan and yet amidst all the diversity  and complexity of ideas practices  experiences values and institutions we  can see that buddhists still have much  in common and that they share especially  a sustained attentiveness to suffering  and  they have suffered just as the Buddha  said about his long career of teaching  both formally and now it is only  suffering and the stopping of suffering  that I described  [Music]  you

